To solve this problem, you can perform a depth-first search (DFS) on the binary tree, starting from the root node. During the search, maintain a running total of the values seen so far on the current path. When a leaf node is reached, check if the running total equals the targetSum. If it does, return true; otherwise, continue the search. You can repeat this process for all possible paths from the root to a leaf node.

Here's a Python implementation:

```
def has_path_sum(root, targetSum):
    if not root:
        return False

    stack = [(root, root.val)]

    while stack:
        node, total = stack.pop()
        if not node.left and not node.right: # if leaf node
            if total == targetSum:
                return True
        if node.left:
            stack.append((node.left, total + node.left.val))
        if node.right:
            stack.append((node.right, total + node.right.val))

    return False
``` 

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the binary tree, and a space complexity of O(h), where h is the height of the tree.
